## Proof

Let $n$ be a composite, so by definition $n = ab$ for some integers $a, b \in \{2, 3, 4, \dots\}$.

Set
$$x = a - 1, \qquad y = b - 1, \qquad z = 1.$$

Since $a, b \geq 2$, we have $x = a-1 \geq 1$ and $y = b-1 \geq 1$, and $z = 1 \geq 1$, so $x, y, z$ are all positive integers.

Now compute:
$$
\begin{aligned}
xy + xz + yz + 1
&= (a-1)(b-1) + (a-1)\cdot 1 + (b-1)\cdot 1 + 1 \\
&= (ab - a - b + 1) + (a - 1) + (b - 1) + 1 \\
&= ab - a - b + 1 + a - 1 + b - 1 + 1 \\
&= ab \\
&= n.
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus every composite $n = ab$ is expressible as $xy + xz + yz + 1$ with positive integers $x, y, z$ (namely $x = a-1$, $y = b-1$, $z = 1$). $\blacksquare$

